1. "A compile-time construct the compiler boils away"
   strong_count before clone 1, after 2
   RefCell borrow_mut while borrowed: Err(BorrowMutError)
   A count that changes and a borrow refused at run time are state the
   program carries. The allocations are counted on the costs page.

2. "String and Vec<T> are smart pointers"
   String derefs to str, Vec<u8> to [u8]
   needs_drop: String true, Vec<u8> true
   String: 'static true, Vec<u8>: 'static true
   Deref and Drop: smart pointers in the sense std's Deref docs use.
   Neither has a lifetime parameter, so the buffer is not held by a
   reference: std stores a raw pointer (a NonNull) and frees it in Drop.

3. "A smart pointer owns the data it refers to"
   Cow::Borrowed(&text) is Borrowed: true
   a Weak outliving its value: upgrade() = None
   Cow::Borrowed owns nothing and a Weak keeps nothing alive; The Book
   says smart pointers own their data "in many cases".

4. "Some raw pointers, such as Rc<T>, offer shared ownership"
   Rc::as_ptr(&counted) is a *const u8
   needs_drop: Rc<u8> true, *const u8 false
   size_of Option<Rc<u8>> 8, Option<*const u8> 16
   Rc is a smart pointer built on a NonNull. A raw pointer is what
   Rc::as_ptr hands you: no count, no drop, and nullable.

5. "Use a smart pointer in place of its referent without fuss"
   boxed.checked_add(1)  Some(42)   a method: found through Deref
   *boxed + 1            42         an operator: needs the *
   `boxed + 1` is E0369. Method calls and coercion sites deref for you;
   operators and patterns do not.

6. Rc is "single-threaded"; Arc is the one that crosses
   thread read Arc<String>: len 4
   strong_count after join: 1
   The same spawn with an Rc is E0277: Rc<String> cannot be sent
   between threads safely.
